{"tool_name":"stitch","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"stitch","home":"http://stitch.embl.de/","summary":"Resource to explore known and predicted interactions of chemicals and proteins. Chemicals are linked to other chemicals and proteins by evidence derived from experiments, databases and the literature.","addition_date":"2016-02-17T09:43:56Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:25:13.779770Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Database portal","Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:stitch","credit":[{"name":"BMBF","typeEntity":"Funding agency","typeRole":["Contributor"],"url":"https://www.bmbf.de/"},{"name":"CPR","typeEntity":"Funding agency","typeRole":["Contributor"],"url":"http://www.cpr.ku.dk/"},{"name":"EC FP6","typeEntity":"Funding agency","typeRole":["Contributor"],"url":"http://ec.europa.eu/research/index.cfm"},{"name":"EMBL","typeEntity":"Funding agency","typeRole":["Contributor"],"url":"http://www.embl.org/"},{"name":"EMBO","typeEntity":"Funding agency","typeRole":["Contributor"],"url":"http://www.embo.org/"},{"name":"HD-HuB","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"],"url":"https://www.hd-hub.de/"},{"name":"Michael Kuhn","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://stitch1.embl.de/cgi/show_info_page.pl"},{"name":"SUND-KU","typeEntity":"Funding agency","typeRole":["Contributor"],"url":"http://healthsciences.ku.dk/"},{"name":"TUD","typeEntity":"Funding agency","typeRole":["Contributor"],"url":"http://www.biotec.tu-dresden.de/"},{"name":"Michael Kuhn","typeRole":["Developer"],"url":"http://stitch1.embl.de/cgi/show_info_page.pl"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://stitch.embl.de/cgi/show_info_page.pl?UserId=pRfi11Qlkhnx&sessionId=sEfmbh_T7QLq"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["larsjuhljensen"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Compound name","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0990"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]},{"data":{"term":"Gene name","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2299"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]},{"data":{"term":"Protein name","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1009"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]},{"data":{"term":"Protein sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2976"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA-like","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2546"}]},{"data":{"term":"Small molecule structure","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1463"},"format":[{"term":"InChIKey","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1199"},{"term":"SMILES","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1196"},{"term":"xls","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3468"}]}],"note":"STITCH is a database of protein \u2013 chemical interactions that integrates many sources of experimental and manually curated evidence with text-mining information and interaction predictions. Its key function is to provide users with predictions about the interactions that their entities of interest (proteins or small molecules) may be making with other such entities, and to visualise where a set of entities of interest can be found within the STITCH network. Direct traceable links to the original sources on which interactions are described in the network, make it easy for users to trace the provenance of the data.","operation":[{"term":"Database search","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2421"},{"term":"Pathway or network analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2497"},{"term":"Pathway or network visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3083"},{"term":"Prediction and recognition","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2423"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Database cross-mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0954"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]},{"data":{"term":"Evidence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2042"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]},{"data":{"term":"Pathway or network","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2600"},"format":[{"term":"Image format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3547"},{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]}]}],"name":"STITCH","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"hdhub","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkm795","metadata":{"abstract":"The knowledge about interactions between proteins and small molecules is essential for the understanding of molecular and cellular functions. However, information on such interactions is widely dispersed across numerous databases and the literature. To facilitate access to this data, STITCH ('search tool for interactions of chemicals') integrates information about interactions from metabolic pathways, crystal structures, binding experiments and drug-target relationships. Inferred information from phenotypic effects, text mining and chemical structure similarity is used to predict relations between chemicals. STITCH further allows exploring the network of chemical relations, also in the context of associated binding proteins. Each proposed interaction can be traced back to the original data sources. Our database contains interaction information for over 68 000 different chemicals, including 2200 drugs, and connects them to 1.5 million genes across 373 genomes and their interactions contained in the STRING database. STITCH is available at http://stitch.embl.de/. \u00a9 2007 The Author(s).","authors":[{"name":"Bork P."},{"name":"Campillos M."},{"name":"Jensen L.J."},{"name":"Kuhn M."},{"name":"von Mering C."}],"citationCount":754,"date":"2008-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"STITCH: Interaction networks of chemicals and proteins"},"pmcid":"PMC2238848","pmid":"18084021","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkp937","metadata":{"abstract":"Over the last years, the publicly available knowledge on interactions between small molecules and proteins has been steadily increasing. To create a network of interactions, STITCH aims to integrate the data dispersed over the literature and various databases of biological pathways, drug-target relationships and binding affinities. In STITCH 2, the number of relevant interactions is increased by incorporation of BindingDB, PharmGKB and the Comparative Toxicogenomics Database. The resulting network can be explored interactively or used as the basis for large-scale analyses. To facilitate links to other chemical databases, we adopt InChIKeys that allow identification of chemicals with a short, checksum-like string. STITCH 2.0 connects proteins from 630 organisms to over 74 000 different chemicals, including 2200 drugs. STITCH can be accessed at http://stitch.embl.de/. \u00a9 The Author(s) 2009. Published by Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Beyer A."},{"name":"Bork P."},{"name":"Campillos M."},{"name":"Franceschini A."},{"name":"Jensen L.J."},{"name":"Kuhn M."},{"name":"Szklarczyk D."},{"name":"Von Mering C."}],"citationCount":231,"date":"2009-11-06T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"STITCH 2: An interaction network database for small molecules and proteins"},"pmcid":"PMC2808890","pmid":"19897548","type":["Other"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkr1011","metadata":{"abstract":"To facilitate the study of interactions between proteins and chemicals, we have created STITCH, an aggregated database of interactions connecting over 300 000 chemicals and 2.6 million proteins from 1133 organisms. Compared to the previous version, the number of chemicals with interactions and the number of high-confidence interactions both increase 4-fold. The database can be accessed interactively through a web interface, displaying interactions in an integrated network view. It is also available for computational studies through downloadable files and an API. As an extension in the current version, we offer the option to switch between two levels of detail, namely whether stereoisomers of a given compound are shown as a merged entity or as separate entities. Separate display of stereoisomers is necessary, for example, for carbohydrates and chiral drugs. Combining the isomers increases the coverage, as interaction databases and publications found through text mining will often refer to compounds without specifying the stereoisomer. The database is accessible at http://stitch.embl.de/. \u00a9 The Author(s) 2011.","authors":[{"name":"Bork P."},{"name":"Franceschini A."},{"name":"Jensen L.J."},{"name":"Kuhn M."},{"name":"Szklarczyk D."},{"name":"Von Mering C."}],"citationCount":250,"date":"2012-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"STITCH 3: Zooming in on protein-chemical interactions"},"pmcid":"PMC3245073","pmid":"22075997","type":["Other"]},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkt1207","metadata":{"abstract":"STITCH is a database of protein-chemical interactions that integrates many sources of experimental and manually curated evidence with text-mining information and interaction predictions. Available at http://stitch.embl.de, the resulting interaction network includes 390 000 chemicals and 3.6 million proteins from 1133 organisms. Compared with the previous version, the number of high-confidence protein-chemical interactions in human has increased by 45%, to 367 000. In this version, we added features for users to upload their own data to STITCH in the form of internal identifiers, chemical structures or quantitative data. For example, a user can now upload a spreadsheet with screening hits to easily check which interactions are already known. To increase the coverage of STITCH, we expanded the text mining to include full-text articles and added a prediction method based on chemical structures. We further changed our scheme for transferring interactions between species to rely on orthology rather than protein similarity. This improves the performance within protein families, where scores are now transferred only to orthologous proteins, but not to paralogous proteins. STITCH can be accessed with a web-interface, an API and downloadable files. \u00a9 2013 The Author(s). Published by Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Blicher T.H."},{"name":"Bork P."},{"name":"Jensen L.J."},{"name":"Kuhn M."},{"name":"Pletscher-Frankild S."},{"name":"Szklarczyk D."},{"name":"Von Mering C."}],"citationCount":413,"date":"2014-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"STITCH 4: Integration of protein-chemical interactions with user data"},"pmcid":"PMC3964996","pmid":"24293645","type":["Other"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Biomedical science","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3344"},{"term":"Chemistry","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3314"},{"term":"Molecular interactions, pathways and networks","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0602"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"STITCH","home":"https://bio.tools/stitch","summary":"Resource to explore known and predicted interactions of chemicals and proteins. Chemicals are linked to other chemicals and proteins by evidence derived from experiments, databases and the literature.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:stitch/cmd/stitch.embl.de","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":137,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2023"},{"count":1,"year":"2026"},{"count":3,"year":"2025"},{"count":5,"year":"2024"},{"count":7,"year":"2012"},{"count":7,"year":"2017"},{"count":8,"year":"2021"},{"count":9,"year":"2018"},{"count":9,"year":"2019"},{"count":9,"year":"2022"},{"count":10,"year":"2016"},{"count":10,"year":"2020"},{"count":17,"year":"2015"},{"count":20,"year":"2014"},{"count":21,"year":"2013"}],"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkr1011","pmcid":"PM